= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the ordinary real <inner product> $\langle u,v\rangle=\int_a^buv\,dx$ on the domain of functions obeying the two separated boundary conditions. <Integration by parts> gives the <Lagrange identity for a Sturm-Liouville operator>
$$
\langle u,Lv\rangle-\langle Lu,v\rangle=\big[p(vu'-uv')\big]_a^b.
$$
At $a$, the two boundary-value vectors $(u(a),u'(a))$ and $(v(a),v'(a))$ satisfy the same homogeneous linear relation. If $A_2=0$, both function values vanish; if $A_2\ne0$, their derivatives are the same constant multiple $-A_1/A_2$ of their values. Either way $vu'-uv'=0$. The same argument applies at $b$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\langle u,Lv\rangle=\langle Lu,v\rangle.}
$$
This proves symmetry, and the regular separated <Sturm-Liouville problem> has the same adjoint domain, so $L$ is <self-adjoint>. To see the domain point, require the boundary form to vanish against every admissible $u$: the independently variable endpoint data force an adjoint-domain function to obey those same endpoint relations.

As usual, each endpoint pair $(A_1,A_2)$ and $(B_1,B_2)$ must be nonzero, and the coefficients must have the regularity needed for these operations. An identically zero pair would impose no condition at that endpoint and would not give the claimed <self-adjoint operator>. The related operator $\omega^{-1}L$ is self-adjoint in the <weighted inner product> $\int_a^b\omega uv\,dx$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Multiply the <eigenvalue equation> of the <Sturm-Liouville problem> by $y$ and integrate. <Integration by parts> gives
$$
\lambda\int_a^b\omega y^2\,dx=\int_a^byLy\,dx=-[pyy']_a^b+\int_a^b\big[p(y')^2+qy^2\big]dx.
$$
Since $y$ is a nonzero <eigenfunction> and $\omega>0$, the denominator is strictly positive. Thus the <Rayleigh quotient> is
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac{\displaystyle\int_a^b[p(y')^2+qy^2]dx-[pyy']_a^b}{\displaystyle\int_a^b\omega y^2\,dx}.}
$$
The endpoint term must be retained for a general <Robin boundary condition>; it vanishes for homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> but need not vanish in the present general problem.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The left boundary condition is $y(0)=0$, while the right one is $y'(\ell)=-ky(\ell)$. Hence $[yy']_0^\ell=-ky(\ell)^2$. The <Rayleigh quotient> becomes
$$
\lambda\int_0^\ell y^2\,dx=\int_0^\ell\big[(y')^2+qy^2\big]dx+ky(\ell)^2.
$$
Every term on the right is nonnegative. If the whole expression were zero, in particular $\int_0^\ell(y')^2dx=0$, so $y$ would be constant. Its zero value at the left endpoint would force $y\equiv0$, contrary to its being an <eigenfunction>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda>0.}
$$
This is the positivity mechanism for <Dirichlet-Robin eigenvalues on a finite interval>. The sign of the boundary contribution is positive for the convention $y'+ky=0$ with $k>0$.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

By part (c), write $\lambda=\mu^2$ with $\mu>0$. The equation is $y''+\mu^2y=0$, and the left <Dirichlet boundary condition> eliminates the cosine term. Thus $y=A\sin(\mu x)$, with $A\ne0$. The right <Robin boundary condition> becomes
$$
\mu\cos(\mu\ell)+k\sin(\mu\ell)=0.
$$
Put $z=\mu\ell$ and $\kappa=k\ell>0$. A root cannot have $\cos z=0$, so this is equivalent to $\tan z=-z/\kappa$.

On each interval $((n-1/2)\pi,n\pi)$, $n\geq1$, the function $\tan z+z/\kappa$ rises from $-\infty$ to a positive value and has derivative $\sec^2z+1/\kappa>0$. The <intermediate value theorem> and strict <monotonicity> therefore give exactly one root $z_n$ there. On the intervening positive-tangent intervals both terms are positive, so there are no further positive roots. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\lambda_n=\frac{z_n^2}{\ell^2},\qquad y_n(x)=A_n\sin\frac{z_nx}{\ell},\qquad(n-\tfrac12)\pi<z_n<n\pi,\quad n=1,2,\ldots.}
$$
These intervals establish $\lambda_1<\lambda_2<\cdots$ and an infinite sequence of <eigenvalues>. The apparent algebraic root $z=0$ of the sine equation is excluded: it gives a zero sine function, and the direct zero-eigenvalue solution $y=Ax$ would require $A(1+k\ell)=0$.

\Image[paper-2-2016-ib-robin-roots.png]
{title=One intersection of $\tan z$ with $-z/\kappa$ on each negative-tangent branch; the illustration uses $\kappa=1$}

For <vector normalization> to unit <norm>, the <Dirichlet-Robin eigenvalues on a finite interval> give
$$
A_n=\left[\frac\ell2+\frac{k}{2(k^2+\lambda_n)}\right]^{-1/2}.
$$
The eigenfunctions can otherwise be scaled by any nonzero real constant.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For fixed $k$ and $\ell$, the line $-z/\kappa$ becomes increasingly negative, so each intersection approaches its branch’s left-hand vertical asymptote. Write $z_n=a_n+\varepsilon_n$, where $a_n=(n-1/2)\pi$ and $\varepsilon_n>0$. The eigenvalue equation gives
$$
\kappa\cot\varepsilon_n=a_n+\varepsilon_n.
$$
Since $\cot\varepsilon=\varepsilon^{-1}-\varepsilon/3+O(\varepsilon^3)$, it follows that $\varepsilon_n=\kappa/a_n+O(a_n^{-3})$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\lambda_n=\frac{(n-1/2)^2\pi^2}{\ell^2}+\frac{2k}{\ell}+O(n^{-2}),\qquad\lambda_n\sim\frac{n^2\pi^2}{\ell^2}.}
$$
The half-integer phase identifies the sharper high-frequency behaviour of these <Dirichlet-Robin eigenvalues on a finite interval>. The expansion holds with the Robin coefficient fixed; it is not a uniform expansion in $k\to\infty$.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <separation of variables>, let $\Phi=S(\sigma)T(\tau)$. The <Laplace equation> gives
$$
-\frac{S''}{S}=\frac{T''}{T}=\lambda.
$$
A nonzero $2\pi$-periodic angular solution requires $\lambda=n^2$, $n=0,1,\ldots$. For $n\geq1$, $S$ is a linear combination of $\cos(n\sigma)$ and $\sin(n\sigma)$, while $T''-n^2T=0$ gives hyperbolic sine and cosine. For $n=0$, periodicity makes $S$ constant, and $T=A_0+B_0\tau$. Negative separation constants would give nonperiodic real exponentials in $\sigma$.

Superposition yields
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\Phi(\sigma,\tau)={}&A_0+B_0\tau\\
&+\sum_{n=1}^\infty\Big\{[A_n\cosh(n\tau)+B_n\sinh(n\tau)]\cos(n\sigma)\\
&\hspace{40mm}+[C_n\cosh(n\tau)+D_n\sinh(n\tau)]\sin(n\sigma)\Big\}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Completeness follows by expanding a <harmonic function> in a <Fourier series> in the periodic coordinate: its <Fourier coefficients> must satisfy exactly these radial equations. As usual, the coefficients are restricted by the convergence required of the series on compact subregions. The <bipolar coordinates> domain imposes no boundary condition at $\tau=0$ or $\tau=\infty$, so both hyperbolic branches remain available.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The only nonzero angular <Fourier mode> is $\cos(2\sigma)$. Its radial equation is $T''-4T=0$. A solution vanishing at $\tau=a$ is proportional to $\sinh[2(\tau-a)]$; making its value one at $b$ fixes the constant. The <Dirichlet problem between bipolar coordinate circles> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi(\sigma,\tau)=\frac{\sinh[2(\tau-a)]}{\sinh[2(b-a)]}\cos(2\sigma).}
$$
Its second derivatives in $\sigma$ and $\tau$ cancel, and the endpoint values are respectively zero and $\cos(2\sigma)$. The difference of any two solutions would be a <harmonic function> with zero <Dirichlet boundary conditions>, so the <maximum principle> proves <uniqueness>.

**Geometric wording.** In the printed <bipolar coordinates>, the circle radius $1/\sinh\tau$ decreases with $\tau$. For $0<a<b$, the $b$ circle is contained inside the $a$ circle. Thus the source’s “inner” and “outer” labels are interchanged geometrically. The solution above follows the explicit coordinate boundary values, which unambiguously specify the problem.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the <product rule> to the <vector field> $F=\phi\nabla\psi-\psi\nabla\phi$:
$$
\nabla\cdot F=\nabla\phi\cdot\nabla\psi+\phi\nabla^2\psi-\nabla\psi\cdot\nabla\phi-\psi\nabla^2\phi=\phi\nabla^2\psi-\psi\nabla^2\phi.
$$
The two mixed <dot products> cancel. The <divergence theorem>, using the outward <unit normal>, then gives <Green's second identity>:
$$
\boxed{\int_V(\phi\nabla^2\psi-\psi\nabla^2\phi)\,dV=\int_S(\phi\,n\cdot\nabla\psi-\psi\,n\cdot\nabla\phi)\,dS.}
$$
The usual assumptions are a sufficiently regular boundary and fields with the derivatives and integrability needed for the theorem; singular sources can instead be treated by excising small balls or using <distributions>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Define the candidate potential by convolving the decaying <Green function> with the source. Acting with the <Laplacian> in the observation variable gives, in the sense of <distributions>,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla_{\mathbf r}^2\int_{\mathbb R^3}G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')\rho(\mathbf r')\,d^3r'
&=-\int_{\mathbb R^3}\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')\rho(\mathbf r')\,d^3r'\\
&=-\rho(\mathbf r).
\end{aligned}
$$
The final equality is the sifting property of the <Dirac delta>. The kernel found in part (ii) is locally <integrable>, and its convolution with a sufficiently regular rapidly decaying source also vanishes at spatial infinity. Thus it satisfies the <Poisson equation> and the required condition at infinity.

The difference from any other regular decaying solution is a <harmonic function> on all of space vanishing at infinity, hence is zero by the <maximum principle>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\phi(\mathbf r)=\int_{\mathbb R^3}G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')\rho(\mathbf r')\,d^3r'.}
$$
One can also obtain this expression from <Green's second identity> on larger and larger balls, with the singularity of the kernel treated distributionally. The surface term at infinity vanishes for the usual $1/r$ potential decay and $1/r^2$ gradient decay.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Translation and rotation invariance allow a radial <Green function> $G(R)$, with $R=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|$. Outside the source, $G''+2G'/R=0$, so $G=A/R+B$. Decay at infinity gives $B=0$.

Integrate the defining equation over a small ball centred on the source. The <divergence theorem> fixes its flux:
$$
\int_{R<\varepsilon}\nabla^2G\,dV=4\pi\varepsilon^2G'(\varepsilon)=-4\pi A=-1.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=\frac1{4\pi|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}.}
$$
This is the <Green function of the Laplacian> with the stated negative-delta sign. A homogeneous $1/R$ addition would change the delta normalization, so the singular coefficient is fixed, not an arbitrary integration constant.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $r=|\mathbf r|$, $s=|\mathbf r'|$. For a spherical source, the angular integral needed for the <Green function of the Laplacian> is
$$
\int\frac{d\Omega'}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}=2\pi\int_{-1}^1\frac{du}{\sqrt{r^2+s^2-2rsu}}=\frac{4\pi}{\max(r,s)}.
$$
This is the angular form of the <spherical shell theorem>. Substituting the source into the <Green function> integral therefore yields
$$
\phi(r)=-\frac1r\int_0^rs e^{-\beta s}\,ds-\int_r^\infty e^{-\beta s}\,ds.
$$
The elementary integrals are
$$
\int_0^rs e^{-\beta s}\,ds=\frac{1-(1+\beta r)e^{-\beta r}}{\beta^2},\qquad\int_r^\infty e^{-\beta s}\,ds=\frac{e^{-\beta r}}\beta.
$$
The terms proportional to $e^{-\beta r}/\beta$ cancel, leaving
$$
\boxed{\phi(r)=\frac{e^{-\beta r}-1}{\beta^2r},\qquad\phi(0)=-\frac1\beta.}
$$
The value at the origin is the continuous limit. At large radius $\phi\sim-1/(\beta^2r)$, consistent with the integrated source $\int\rho\,dV=-4\pi/\beta^2$ and the factor $1/(4\pi r)$ in the far-field potential.

For a direct check, set $u=r\phi$. Then $u''=e^{-\beta r}$, so $\nabla^2\phi=u''/r=e^{-\beta r}/r=-\rho$ for $r>0$. Boundedness of $\phi$ at zero cancels the singular $1/r$ terms and excludes an extra point source. The original density is itself singular but locally <integrable>; the equation at the origin is understood in the sense of <distributions>. This is the <Poisson potential of an exponential-over-radius source> with amplitude $A=-1$.

