= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take the <Fourier transform> convention $\widetilde y(\omega)=\int_{\mathbb R}y(t)e^{-i\omega t}\,dt$, with inverse factor $e^{i\omega t}/(2\pi)$. Then $\widetilde{y^{(n)}}=(i\omega)^n\widetilde y$, classically when the boundary terms vanish or in the sense of <tempered distributions>.

Multiply the given frequency relation by its denominator and then by $-i$:
$$
(-i\omega^3-\omega^2-4i\omega-4)\widetilde y=i\omega\widetilde f.
$$
Recognizing the four derivative multipliers gives
$$
\boxed{y'''+y''-4y'-4y=f',\qquad(D+1)(D-2)(D+2)y=f',\quad D=\frac d{dt}.}
$$
The convention is stated explicitly because reversing the Fourier exponential changes intermediate frequency signs. The distributional form will matter at the jump of $f$ in part (b).

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The inverse <Fourier transform> is
$$
f(t)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb R}\frac{-i\,e^{i\omega t}}{\omega-i}\,d\omega.
$$
For $t>0$, close in the upper half-plane, where $e^{i\omega t}$ decays. The single enclosed <pole> has <residue> $-ie^{-t}$, so counterclockwise integration gives $f=e^{-t}$. For $t<0$, close clockwise in the lower half-plane; there is no enclosed pole and the result is zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{f(t)=e^{-t}H(t),\qquad f(t)=0\ (t<0),\quad f(t)=e^{-t}\ (t>0).}
$$
Here $H$ is the <Heaviside step function>. At the jump, symmetric <Fourier inversion> gives $f(0)=1/2$, the average of the one-sided limits. The transform itself cannot fix a function’s value at a single point, so this is the conventional representative rather than extra frequency information.

A direct check is $\int_0^\infty e^{-(1+i\omega)t}dt=(1+i\omega)^{-1}=-i/(\omega-i)$. Distributionally, $f'=\delta(t)-e^{-t}H(t)$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Factor the cubic denominator as $(\omega-i)(\omega^2+4)$. Substitution gives
$$
\widetilde y(\omega)=\frac{i\omega}{(\omega-i)^2(\omega^2+4)}.
$$
There is a double <pole> at $i$ and simple poles at $2i,-2i$. For $t>0$, close the inverse-transform contour counterclockwise in the upper half-plane. The <residue> at the double pole is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Res}_{\omega=i}\big(\widetilde y e^{i\omega t}\big)
&=\left.\frac d{d\omega}\left(\frac{i\omega e^{i\omega t}}{\omega^2+4}\right)\right|_{\omega=i}\\
&=i\left(\frac59-\frac t3\right)e^{-t},
\end{aligned}
$$
and the residue at $2i$ is $-ie^{-2t}/2$. Multiplication by $i$ from the <residue theorem> therefore gives $y(t)=(t/3-5/9)e^{-t}+e^{-2t}/2$.

For $t<0$, the clockwise lower closure encloses only $-2i$, with residue $-ie^{2t}/18$. The orientation supplies $-i$, giving $y(t)=-e^{2t}/18$. Hence the continuous solution is
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\begin{cases}-\dfrac1{18}e^{2t},&t<0,\\[2pt]-\dfrac1{18},&t=0,\\[2pt]\left(\dfrac t3-\dfrac59\right)e^{-t}+\dfrac12e^{-2t},&t>0.\end{cases}}
$$
The large inverse-transform arcs vanish: the rational factor is $O(\omega^{-3})$, while the exponential is bounded on the appropriate closure.

\Image[paper-2-2016-ib-fourier-solution.png]
{title=The two-sided decaying Fourier solution; it and its first derivative are continuous at zero}

The graph tends to zero from below as $t\to-\infty$, passes through $y(0)=-1/18$ with slope $-1/9$, reaches a small negative minimum after zero, then crosses the axis, reaches a positive maximum and tends to zero from above. The positive-time stationary points solve $8/9-t/3=e^{-t}$, and the crossing solves $t/3-5/9+e^{-t}/2=0$; these equations determine the sketch without guessing signs.

For a distributional check, $y$ and $y'$ are continuous, while $y''(0^+)-y''(0^-)=1$. The <jump conditions for a third-order Green function> therefore produce the delta in $f'=\delta-e^{-t}H(t)$, and each smooth branch satisfies the remaining equation. The nonzero solution for $t<0$ is consistent with the stipulated decay at both temporal infinities. Those are two-sided conditions, not a causality assumption: this is the setting of a <decaying two-sided Green function of a constant-coefficient ordinary differential equation> and <causality versus stability of a Green function>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A second-order Cartesian <tensor> assigns components in each orthonormal coordinate system according to
$$
\boxed{T'_{ij}=R_{ik}R_{j\ell}T_{k\ell},\qquad T'=RTR^T,\qquad R^TR=I.}
$$
Here $R$ is the matrix taking old <vector> components to new ones, and repeated indices are summed. This is the <tensor component transformation law>; an arbitrary array of nine numbers is not a tensor unless its components in different frames obey this rule. No symmetry $T_{ij}=T_{ji}$ is implied by being a second-order tensor.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $R$ be an orthogonal change of axes. Since $A$ is a second-order <tensor>, $A'_{ij}=R_{ik}R_{j\ell}A_{k\ell}$. The scalar-contraction assumption gives
$$
C'_{ij}R_{ik}R_{j\ell}A_{k\ell}=C_{k\ell}A_{k\ell}
$$
for every tensor $A$. In one fixed frame its components can be any array: in particular, choose one component at a time to be one, all others zero. Equality of the coefficients then forces
$$
C'_{ij}R_{ik}R_{j\ell}=C_{k\ell},\qquad R^TC'R=C.
$$
Multiplying by $R$ and $R^T$ yields
$$
\boxed{C'=RCR^T,\qquad C'_{ij}=R_{ik}R_{j\ell}C_{k\ell}.}
$$
This is exactly the <tensor component transformation law>. It is the full-contraction version of the <quotient theorem for Cartesian tensors>. The words “for every” are essential: testing a single $A$, such as the zero tensor or the identity, would not determine all components.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the quarter-turn <rotation matrix>
$$
R=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For a general second-order <tensor>, direct multiplication gives
$$
RTR^T=\begin{pmatrix}T_{22}&-T_{21}&-T_{23}\\-T_{12}&T_{11}&T_{13}\\-T_{32}&T_{31}&T_{33}\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Requiring $RTR^T=T$ gives $T_{11}=T_{22}$, $T_{12}=-T_{21}$, $T_{13}=-T_{23}$ together with $T_{23}=T_{13}$, and $T_{31}=-T_{32}$ together with $T_{32}=T_{31}$. Thus all mixed axial-transverse entries vanish and
$$
\boxed{T=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\omega&0\\-\omega&\alpha&0\\0&0&\beta\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
For an arbitrary rotation angle $\theta$, the transverse rotation is $Q_\theta=\cos\theta\,I+\sin\theta\,J$, where $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$. The transverse block of $T$ is $\alpha I-\omega J$, so it commutes with $Q_\theta$. Therefore $Q_\theta(\alpha I-\omega J)Q_\theta^T=\alpha I-\omega J$, while the axial component $\beta$ is unchanged.

Hence **invariance holds under every rotation about the axis**, not just the quarter-turn. This is the general <axisymmetric second-order tensor>; a <symmetric tensor> would additionally require $\omega=0$, but that restriction was not assumed.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $i\ne j$, the <inertia tensor> component is $I_{ij}=-\rho\int_Vx_ix_j\,dV$. Reflect either coordinate appearing once: the cylindrical domain and density are unchanged, while the integrand changes sign. Thus every off-diagonal component vanishes. Rotational symmetry also implies $I_{11}=I_{22}$.

Use <cylindrical coordinates> $x_1=r\cos\theta$, $x_2=r\sin\theta$, $x_3=z$, with volume element $r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz$. The axial <moment of inertia> is
$$
I_{33}=\rho\int_{-2}^{2}dz\int_0^{2\pi}d\theta\int_0^1r^3\,dr=2\pi\rho.
$$
The two contributions to a transverse component are
$$
\int_Vx_2^2\,dV=4\left(\int_0^1r^3dr\right)\left(\int_0^{2\pi}\sin^2\theta\,d\theta\right)=\pi,
\qquad\int_Vz^2\,dV=\pi\int_{-2}^{2}z^2dz=\frac{16\pi}{3}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{I=\pi\rho\,\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{19}{3},\frac{19}{3},2\right)=M\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{19}{12},\frac{19}{12},\frac12\right),\qquad M=4\pi\rho.}
$$
The axial value $MR^2/2$ and transverse value $M(3R^2+h^2)/12$ for $R=1$, $h=4$ give an independent check on both diagonal entries.

