= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <zero-natural-length spring>, extension is the full distance between its endpoints, so the squared spring lengths are quadratic functions of the Cartesian coordinates. The <Lagrangian> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
L={}&\frac{m_1}{2}(\dot X_1^2+\dot Y_1^2)+\frac{m_2}{2}(\dot X_2^2+\dot Y_2^2)\\
&-\frac{k_1}{2}(X_1^2+Y_1^2)-\frac{k_2}{2}\big[(X_2-X_1)^2+(Y_2-Y_1)^2\big]\\
&-g(m_1Y_1+m_2Y_2).
\end{aligned}}
$$
The gravitational <potential energy> has positive derivative in the upward coordinate, giving downward forces. Applying the <Euler-Lagrange equations> to all four coordinates gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
m_1\ddot X_1+(k_1+k_2)X_1-k_2X_2&=0,\\
m_2\ddot X_2+k_2X_2-k_2X_1&=0,\\
m_1\ddot Y_1+(k_1+k_2)Y_1-k_2Y_2&=-m_1g,\\
m_2\ddot Y_2+k_2Y_2-k_2Y_1&=-m_2g.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The two spring forces are equal and opposite on the two masses for the connecting spring, as required by <Newton's third law>.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At <equilibrium>, the two horizontal equations imply $\hat X_2=\hat X_1$ and then $k_1\hat X_1=0$, so both vanish. Adding the two vertical equations gives $k_1\hat Y_1=-(m_1+m_2)g$; the second separately gives $k_2(\hat Y_2-\hat Y_1)=-m_2g$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\hat X_1=\hat X_2=0,\qquad\hat Y_1=-\frac{(m_1+m_2)g}{k_1},\qquad\hat Y_2=-\frac{(m_1+m_2)g}{k_1}-\frac{m_2g}{k_2}.}
$$
Retain the imposed <constraint> $X_2=0$ and set $X_1=x_1$, $Y_1=\hat Y_1+y_1$, $Y_2=\hat Y_2+y_2$. Subtracting the equilibrium equations cancels all constant gravitational terms. For $q=(x_1,y_1,y_2)^T$, the <small-oscillation mass and stiffness matrices> are
$$
\boxed{M\ddot q+Kq=0,\quad M=\begin{pmatrix}m_1&0&0\\0&m_1&0\\0&0&m_2\end{pmatrix},\quad K=\begin{pmatrix}k_1+k_2&0&0\\0&k_1+k_2&-k_2\\0&-k_2&k_2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The horizontal reaction enforcing the constraint replaces the removed $X_2$ equation; it is not necessary to demand that the original unconstrained horizontal force on mass 2 vanish during motion. Because the <zero-natural-length spring> potentials are exactly quadratic, these displacement equations are actually exact within the ideal spring model, not merely first-order approximations.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Keep the constraint from part (b). For equal masses and spring constants, the <generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations> reduces to
$$
\frac km\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\0&2&-1\\0&-1&1\end{pmatrix}v=\omega^2v.
$$
The horizontal coordinate decouples, giving
$$
\boxed{\omega_x=\sqrt{\frac{2k}{m}},\qquad v_x=(1,0,0)^T.}
$$
In this <normal mode>, mass 1 oscillates horizontally, mass 2 has its horizontal coordinate fixed, and neither mass has a vertical displacement.

For the vertical block, write $\lambda=m\omega^2/k$. Its <characteristic polynomial> is $(2-\lambda)(1-\lambda)-1=\lambda^2-3\lambda+1$. Thus $\lambda_\pm=(3\pm\sqrt5)/2$, and the first vertical equation gives $y_2/y_1=2-\lambda_\pm$. If $\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2$ is the <golden ratio>, the two vertical modes are
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\omega_-&=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}\frac{3-\sqrt5}{2}}=\frac1\varphi\sqrt{\frac km},&v_-&=(0,1,\varphi)^T,\\
\omega_+&=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}\frac{3+\sqrt5}{2}}=\varphi\sqrt{\frac km},&v_+&=(0,1,-1/\varphi)^T.
\end{aligned}}
$$
In the lower-frequency mode, the masses move vertically in phase, with the lower mass having the larger amplitude. In the higher-frequency mode they move oppositely, with lower-to-upper amplitude ratio $1/\varphi$. Their <Euclidean inner product> is zero because $1+\varphi(-1/\varphi)=0$; since $M=mI$, this is also <mass-matrix orthogonality of normal modes>.

The three frequencies satisfy $\omega_-<\omega_x<\omega_+$. Each mode has a scalar coordinate $A\cos\omega t+B\sin\omega t$, and their superposition gives the general constrained motion. No zero-frequency mode remains because the first spring anchors the system. Gravity fixes the equilibrium extensions but drops out of these frequencies for zero-natural-length springs.

