= Solution
{parent=a}

The <quotient group> $G/N$ consists of the <cosets> $gN$, with multiplication
$$
\boxed{(gN)(hN)=(gh)N.}
$$
For well-definedness, replacing representatives by $gn_1$ and $hn_2$ changes their product to $gh(h^{-1}n_1h)n_2$. Since $N$ is a <normal subgroup>, the final two factors lie in $N$, so the product coset does not change.

<Associativity> follows from that in $G$; the <identity element> is the coset $N$, and $(gN)^{-1}=g^{-1}N$. Hence these cosets form a <group>. By <Lagrange's theorem>, every subgroup $H\leq G$ has order dividing $|G|$, and its <subgroup index> is $[G:H]=|G|/|H|$. In particular
$$
\boxed{|G/N|=\frac{|G|}{|N|}.}
$$
Normality is needed for the quotient multiplication, whereas Lagrange’s counting formula holds for every subgroup.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The four displayed matrices fail <closure>: for example $\sigma_x\sigma_y=i\sigma_z$ is not one of them. Thus they do not form a <group> by themselves.

Let $\sigma_0=I$ and $\mu_4=\{1,-1,i,-i\}$. The enlarged set is the single-qubit <Pauli group>
$$
\boxed{\mathcal P_1=\{\eta\sigma_a:\eta\in\mu_4,\ a\in\{0,x,y,z\}\}.}
$$
There are 16 distinct matrices: $I,\sigma_x,\sigma_y,\sigma_z$ are linearly independent, so two different ones cannot be scalar multiples, and the four phases are distinct for each nonzero matrix.

The <Pauli matrices> satisfy
$$
\sigma_a\sigma_b=\delta_{ab}I+i\epsilon_{abc}\sigma_c\quad(a,b\in\{x,y,z\}),\qquad\sigma_a^2=I.
$$
Here $\delta$ is the <Kronecker delta> and $\epsilon$ the <Levi-Civita symbol>. Thus any product of two Pauli matrices is another Pauli matrix or the identity times a phase in $\mu_4$. Multiplying by the two original phases preserves membership in $\mu_4$, proving <closure> of all 16 matrices.

<Associativity> is inherited from <matrix multiplication>, the identity is $I$, and
$$
(\eta\sigma_a)^{-1}=\eta^{-1}\sigma_a\in\mathcal P_1.
$$
This proves every group axiom. The resulting group is non-Abelian, since reversing $\sigma_x\sigma_y$ changes its sign.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For every positive integer $m$, the group laws give $(g^{-1})^m=(g^m)^{-1}$. Consequently
$$
g^m=e\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad(g^{-1})^m=e.
$$
The sets of positive exponents returning the two elements to the <identity element> are identical. If this set is nonempty, its least member is the <order of a group element> in both cases. If it is empty, neither element has finite order. Therefore, including the infinite-order case,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(g^{-1})=\operatorname{ord}(g).}
$$
For a direct divisibility argument in the finite case, if $g$ has order $n$, then $(g^{-1})^n=e$, so its inverse’s order divides $n$. Reversing the roles shows $n$ divides that order, forcing equality. This is the general fact that <an element and its inverse have the same order>; it also reflects $\langle g\rangle=\langle g^{-1}\rangle$ as <cyclic subgroups>.

