Write $H=\{e,h\}$ with $h\ne e$. For any $g\in G$, the <normal subgroup> property implies $ghg^{-1}\in H$. This conjugate cannot be $e$, since multiplication by $g^{-1}$ and $g$ would then give $h=e$. There is only one remaining choice, so
$$
ghg^{-1}=h,\qquad gh=hg.
$$
Thus $h$ commutes with every element of $G$; the identity does too. Hence
$$
\boxed{H\subseteq Z(G).}
$$
This proves that a <normal subgroup of order two is central>. The proof does not require $G$ to be finite: conjugation permutes $H$ and fixes its identity, so it must fix its only other element.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <group homomorphism> $\phi:H\to G$ is a map preserving multiplication:
$$
\boxed{\phi(h_1h_2)=\phi(h_1)\phi(h_2)\quad\text{for all }h_1,h_2\in H.}
$$
It necessarily sends $e_H$ to $e_G$ and sends inverses to inverses, by applying this relation to $e_H^2$ and $hh^{-1}$. Its <kernel of a group homomorphism> is
$$
\boxed{\ker\phi=\{h\in H:\phi(h)=e_G\}.}
$$
The kernel is a <normal subgroup> of the domain: if $k\in\ker\phi$ and $h\in H$, then $\phi(hkh^{-1})=\phi(h)e_G\phi(h)^{-1}=e_G$. The kernel’s identity condition refers to the identity in the target group.

= c
{parent=9}
